WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:06.440
So in the previous section, Phillip
explained why

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.350
the problem of multiple pattern matching
is important.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.070
And he further gave an algorithm

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.470
for solving the multiple pattern matching
problem.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.540
But let's see, what is the way in which he
solved the problem?

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.720
Here is the genome as the road, and here
is the pattern as a student.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.490
So basically what Phillip does, is he
asked the student to go

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.960
along the road and find all the matches
that a

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.170
pattern can match.
But please remember that

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:50.750
we have multiple patterns.
So this approach force us

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:56.020
to slide the patterns along the genome
multiple times.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.200
OK?
Here we slide.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:09.020
This approach takes a lot of time.
The time complexity of this algorithm

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:15.230
is O( |genome size| * |Patterns| ).

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.110
In brute force, the combined length of all
the patterns, is huge.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.090
And this, algorithm is not that practical.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:33.280
Now, I'm describing another approach, so
instead of asking

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.390
individual patterns to go along the
genome, one at a time

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:44.400
I pack all of the patterns into a single

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.120
structure and move this structure along
the genome only once.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.720
So, it is similar to packing all the
students onto a bus

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.820
and move the bus just one on the road.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.680
This data structure has a very important
property.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.830
It can retrieve the pattern very quickly.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.260
Like, if a father here asks if Taca is on
this bus?

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.860
The bus driver can say "I can retrieve
Taca" very quickly.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:33.161
So, this data structure is called a
Trie, coming from the word retrieval.

00:02:33.161 --> 00:02:39.530
So the Trie is a data structure for
representing a collection of patterns.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.320
And the tries support fast pattern
matching.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.620
Now, given us a set of patterns, the

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.820
idea is to combine all these patterns into

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.890
a rooted tree, with branches labeled by
letters in the alphabet.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:05.540
And every string in the patterns
correspond to a path from the root to a leaf.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.800
Let's go through this example with me, to
understand how we can construct a trie

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.890
from a collection of patterns.
OK?

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.910
We start with a root and the first pattern
is banana.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.990
So from the root the first character of
banana is b.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:32.360
There is no b here so we have to create a
new branch and label it my b.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.530
The next character is a.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:42.042
So we have to create a new branch and
label it by a, and so on, so fourth.

00:03:42.042 --> 00:03:46.800
An a.
So for the next pattern, p-a-n.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.560
We start from the root, there's no p.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.450
So we have to create a new branch p here,
OK?

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.390
And a, n.
The next one is "and".

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.260
We start from the root, there's no "and"
going down from the root, so we have to

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:10.250
create one, 'a' and the next one is n-d.
Next one is n-a-b, "nab".

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.470
We start from the root.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:18.292
We don't have 'n' so we have to create a new
one and a-b.

00:04:18.292 --> 00:04:24.140
OK?
Antenna, we start from the root.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.330
But please note that' a' here we have a
branch

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.350
labelled by 'a', so we don't have to
create new one.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:31.586
We just follow it down.

00:04:31.586 --> 00:04:35.186
Go to next 'n', here the next one is 't'.

00:04:35.186 --> 00:04:39.056
We don't have' t', so we have to create a
new one.

00:04:39.056 --> 00:04:45.140
And e-n-n-a.
The next one is bandana.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.090
We start from the root.
We have b already,

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:55.060
so we don't have to create a new one.
So, a-n, there's no d going

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.360
down, so we have to create a new one.
And a.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.360
Next one is ananas.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.034
We, we have a already, so we just follow
down.

00:05:05.034 --> 00:05:09.844
And here 'a', we don't have 'a', we have to
create a new 'a'.

00:05:09.844 --> 00:05:10.478
OK?

00:05:10.478 --> 00:05:14.730
n-a-s.
The next one is nana.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:20.950
We start from the root.
"nana": we go through a n-a, there

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:26.835
is no 'n' next, so we have to create a new
one, here right.

00:05:26.835 --> 00:05:27.173
OK?

00:05:27.173 --> 00:05:33.270
So this tree, this rooted tree is called a

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:39.740
trie, constructed from a set of patterns.
So a trie is a

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.470
rooted tree with branches labelled by
letters in the alphabet.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:50.740
Every pattern is a path
from the root to the leaf.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:56.050
For example, banana.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:01.910
It correspond to path from the root to the
leaf.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:05.230
But please note that in this example, we
have some errors here.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.240
If you want to construct a trie for
yourself, you need

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:14.320
to add a new characters dollar at the end
of each pattern.
(Denoting an end of a word in the trie.)

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.050
We don't have space so allow us to have
some mistake here.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:26.690
So, the question now is, how to use the
trie for pattern matching?

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.130
Before, Phillip had to move individual
pattern, one

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.970
at a time, along genome, to find a match.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:41.120
Now, we have already created a special
structure called a

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.530
trie that packes all the patterns into a
single data structure.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.660
Now the ultimate thing we have to do

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.526
is to move this data structure along the
genome.

00:06:52.526 --> 00:06:54.381
OK.

00:06:56.450 --> 00:07:02.952
Here we have the string, that's the
genome, panamabananas.

00:07:02.952 --> 00:07:09.270
The trie, constructed from the set
of patterns we want to match.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.230
First we put the trie at the first
position of the string.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.950
P, and we want to ask a question,

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:22.970
whether there is any pattern in this
connection

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:27.000
that can match at this position on the
genome.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.520
So we just swap down from the root nearest
p, so this is a p-match.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.190
We walk down a-n.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:36.120
So good.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.680
So at this position we can find a match:
"pan".

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:47.030
We move the trie to the next position,
so, and we try to walk

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:52.960
down the tree from the root to the leaf of
the tree, to find if there is any match.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.974
So, we could find 'a' here, next one is 'n'.

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:58.860
Next one is 'a' here, yes we can find.
Next one is 'm'.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.680
No.
In the trie, we don't have anything.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:06.969
Going out from this we don't have label 'm'

00:08:06.969 --> 00:08:11.100
so at this position we cannot find any match.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.562
We move the trie, one more time.

00:08:14.562 --> 00:08:21.240
An n here a-n we cannot find n, so that is
no match at this position.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:24.146
We move to the next.

00:08:24.146 --> 00:08:29.640
We start with a-n, here we got stuck we
cannot match so we have to move.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.052
We can say that at this position there is
no path

00:08:32.052 --> 00:08:32.400
that matches.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.666
This position immediately there is no
match.

00:08:35.666 --> 00:08:41.165
Next position 'a' here, there is no match
here,

00:08:41.165 --> 00:08:46.249
we move to the next b-a-n-a-n-a-s.

00:08:46.249 --> 00:08:48.980
OK, so we can find a match here.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:56.164
So we move it to the next one,
a-n-a-n-a-s.

00:08:56.164 --> 00:08:59.400
Exactly.
So we can find a match at this position.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:06.833
So next one is n-a-n-a.
We can find a match.

00:09:06.833 --> 00:09:14.340
So we move next, a-n-a-s.
There is no 's', so walking down.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:19.110
So at this position we cannot find any
path that matches.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.650
Similarly, we move the trie,

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:23.870
here we cannot find any match, here we

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:27.506
cannot find match, here, immediately we
cannot match.

00:09:27.506 --> 00:09:28.733
OK?

00:09:28.733 --> 00:09:34.060
So, to recap.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.890
In this section I have described a data
structure called a trie.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.610
Which is a representation of a collection
of strings.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:47.160
This data structure allows us to retrieve
a pattern from

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.750
the collection very quickly.
And the run time of the trie-matching, now

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:59.260
we have reduced it to the genome
multiplied by the longest pattern.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:03.160
Where in the previous algorithm that
Phillip described,

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:06.420
it takes genome multiplied by
the total length of all the patterns.

